WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.609
My name is Daniel Colon-Ramos, I am an assistant

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:12.745
professor at Yale University and my lab is interested

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:16.382
in understanding how the architecture of structures

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:22.722
like the brain forms. And I spend most of my time thinking about

00:00:22.723 --> 00:00:25.892
these questions, but today I wanted to do something a little bit

00:00:25.893 --> 00:00:30.363
different. It actually doesn't have to do directly with research,

00:00:30.364 --> 00:00:34.867
but I wanted to use the brain and you know, some general

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:39.071
musings, to basically to think about what can we learn

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:44.877
about the brain with regards to how we learn, how we generate

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:49.015
knowledge, and also how science works. So what I'm going to be doing

00:00:49.016 --> 00:00:52.952
today, I'm going to jump into different topics. And again, this is not

00:00:52.953 --> 00:00:55.788
a science talk, it's more like a general thought talk.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:01:00.560
But I'm going to illustrate how we think about science and

00:01:00.561 --> 00:01:05.031
through collaborations, through creating networks of knowledge,

00:01:05.032 --> 00:01:08.401
we basically, as scientists, generate new knowledge. And in that,

00:01:08.402 --> 00:01:12.605
I'm going to illustrate the importance of cooperativity and the importance

00:01:12.606 --> 00:01:15.007
of collaboration, which is a topic that's seldom discussed

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:19.645
in the sciences. But is essential for creating new knowledge

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:23.649
and for furthering the sciences, in general.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:30.089
So, the brain is made out of cells that are called neurons.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:36.329
And there are more neurons in a single human brain

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:41.033
than there are stars in the whole Milky Way. So there are about

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:43.803
a hundred billion neurons. So if there are some out there that are

00:01:43.804 --> 00:01:46.172
watching this talk, next time that you are sitting down and looking at

00:01:46.173 --> 00:01:49.575
the sky, think that there are more neurons in your brain than there

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:55.014
are stars you are seeing in the skies. And basically, there are a lot

00:01:55.015 --> 00:01:59.485
of them. And they connect in a very specific way. And they actually

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:05.324
underlay human behaviors, they underlay the capacity to communicate

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:10.563
for you to understand the stock, and the organization is critical in that

00:02:10.564 --> 00:02:14.901
aspect. So we're interested in this question, in how we

00:02:14.902 --> 00:02:19.438
said that the architecture of the brain develops. And my lab

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:23.576
in particular, pursues this question by using the nematode, C elegans,

00:02:23.577 --> 00:02:27.980
as a model organism. C. elegans is a tiny, tiny worm. It's about the size

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:31.350
of a comma in a sentence. So some of you might be asking me, how

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:34.453
is it that you can claim that you understand how the human brain,

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.323
which is so complex, works by looking at such a tiny worm?

00:02:37.324 --> 00:02:40.626
And I want to explain a little bit how we do that, because

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:43.563
this is actually how -- the way that we do it, and the way that

00:02:43.564 --> 00:02:46.499
other colleagues in the field that use C. elegans or use other

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.504
model organisms do it, actually highlights how science is done and how

00:02:51.505 --> 00:02:56.542
knowledge is generated. So, C. elegans as I mentioned is really

00:02:56.543 --> 00:03:00.713
small. And the reason that we use it is because scientists have

00:03:00.714 --> 00:03:05.718
created tools that allow us to examine questions that will otherwise

00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:09.222
be very, very complicated to examine. Questions like how is it that

00:03:09.223 --> 00:03:13.526
you deconstruct the architecture of a structure like the brain.

00:03:13.527 --> 00:03:17.830
And in particular, we use C. elegans because it's the only animal

00:03:17.831 --> 00:03:21.200
for which we know the wiring diagram of the whole nervous system.

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:25.771
So, somebody sat down and mapped out all of the neurons, all of the

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:29.876
cells that form part of the nervous system, and so we know who they're

00:03:29.877 --> 00:03:32.979
connecting to, where they're positioned, and for many of them,

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.648
we know what behaviors they're important for. And all that knowledge

00:03:35.649 --> 00:03:38.351
is actually very important, because we use that knowledge

00:03:38.352 --> 00:03:41.754
to generate and create new knowledge. So why is that knowledge

00:03:41.755 --> 00:03:43.589
important? And the reason that that knowledge is important is

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.726
because of evolution. So it's thanks to evolution that a lot of

00:03:46.727 --> 00:03:51.163
the concepts that we find in, for example, C. elegans, they're actually

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:55.434
applicable to other organisms, to other animals, including ourselves.

00:03:55.435 --> 00:04:00.072
And so much so, that in the past 10 years, 6 Nobel prizes

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:02.808
have been awarded to people working in C. elegans that have

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:05.645
made fundamental discoveries in C. elegans, which were then

00:04:05.646 --> 00:04:11.951
widely applicable to other systems, which furthered our general

00:04:11.952 --> 00:04:15.955
knowledge of how biology works. And they were recognized with

00:04:15.956 --> 00:04:19.091
Nobel prizes. But the important part beyond the prize is that

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:21.861
because of evolution, we can use model organisms, be it

00:04:21.862 --> 00:04:26.599
C. elegans or yeast or flies or mice, to be able to generate fundamental

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:34.373
knowledge that then can benefit humanity. So, that knowledge has

00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:38.144
told us something fundamental, very important, about how the nervous

00:04:38.145 --> 00:04:44.183
system works. Be it C. elegans or our own brains, neurons connect

00:04:44.184 --> 00:04:49.188
to each other and cooperate to form networks. And that cooperativity

00:04:49.189 --> 00:04:52.992
between these individual cells is what basically underlies the

00:04:52.993 --> 00:04:55.928
power of the nervous system, the power of our own brains.

00:04:55.929 --> 00:05:00.066
So neuron isolation is not that powerful, but in combination with

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:02.935
other neurons, it's capable of creating all these circuits which then

00:05:02.936 --> 00:05:06.973
are capable of doing amazing things, like creating a concept of

00:05:06.974 --> 00:05:13.145
reality and allowing communication. So, but one important aspect

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:19.552
is that the number of neurons is important, but what is more critical

00:05:19.553 --> 00:05:22.321
than the number of the neurons is how they're organized.

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:26.926
So, much like having a bunch of cables does not make a computer,

00:05:26.927 --> 00:05:30.496
having a bunch of neurons that are disorganized does not make

00:05:30.497 --> 00:05:33.099
a nervous system. They have to be organized in a particular

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.434
way. And again, I want to bring as an example, C. elegans.

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:40.506
C. elegans only has 302 neurons, so that's far fewer neurons

00:05:40.507 --> 00:05:42.975
than 100 billion neurons that you find in a single human brain.

00:05:42.976 --> 00:05:47.380
But those 302 neurons are organized in such a way that allows

00:05:47.381 --> 00:05:50.950
the animal to create a representation of its world to find mates,

00:05:50.951 --> 00:05:56.088
to avoid predators, to find food. And that's what we're interested

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.958
in. We're interested in understanding how is it that those neurons

00:05:58.959 --> 00:06:01.961
get organized, and how that organization then underlies the behavior

00:06:01.962 --> 00:06:08.935
of the nematode. The human brain is obviously organized in a different

00:06:08.936 --> 00:06:12.305
way, because we don't have the same pressures. The same evolutionary

00:06:12.306 --> 00:06:15.374
pressures or the same needs as C. elegans. So we are -- our brains

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:19.212
are wired in a different way than the C. elegans brain, thankfully.

00:06:19.213 --> 00:06:24.083
And one critical aspect of our brain, which is fascinating, at least to me,

00:06:24.084 --> 00:06:27.720
is that our brains are actually wired to be wired. And what I mean

00:06:27.721 --> 00:06:33.025
by that is that when C. elegans are born, or when reptiles are born,

00:06:33.026 --> 00:06:36.562
they know what to do. It's almost like their behaviors are wired

00:06:36.563 --> 00:06:41.100
and they know where to go. But when we're born, a new baby is born,

00:06:41.101 --> 00:06:46.739
it's actually -- it looks like what Aristotle used to call a tabula rasa.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.944
It looks like the mind is empty. It's not really empty, it's wired to achieve

00:06:51.945 --> 00:06:55.147
something very important. And that something very important is to connect

00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:57.316
to other humans, and to learn from other humans. That's what we

00:06:57.317 --> 00:07:02.088
are connected to. And that capacity of our brain to connect to

00:07:02.089 --> 00:07:04.657
other people, to be able to learn from others, to be able to

00:07:04.658 --> 00:07:07.493
build on knowledge that was generated by others before us,

00:07:07.494 --> 00:07:13.399
is fundamental for the sciences. So, knowledge to a certain extent,

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:17.637
can be considered a network of networks. Because our brain is

00:07:17.638 --> 00:07:21.007
a network, and the knowledge that we're generating by connecting our

00:07:21.008 --> 00:07:25.711
brains to other people is a network of networks. So, let me give you

00:07:25.712 --> 00:07:28.714
an example of how this works in the real science world.

00:07:28.715 --> 00:07:33.686
So, I became interested in understanding how the nervous system

00:07:33.687 --> 00:07:38.624
of the nematode develops in embryos. But I didn't have the tools to

00:07:38.625 --> 00:07:43.462
be able to do that, so I collaborated --  I linked my brain to

00:07:43.463 --> 00:07:47.433
other experts that had tools that were very beneficial to my

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:49.869
research, and my research was beneficial to theirs.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:53.072
One of them is Zhirong Bao, who is at Sloan Kettering.

00:07:53.073 --> 00:07:57.009
He's a computer scientist turned developmental biologist.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:00.813
And he developed a computer that he trained to be able to

00:08:00.814 --> 00:08:05.218
observe, basically here you have a nematode embryo, where the nuclei

00:08:05.219 --> 00:08:09.455
in the cells, the center of the cells, are labeled in green. And the computer

00:08:09.456 --> 00:08:13.292
is going to basically visualize this embryo as it's developing

00:08:13.293 --> 00:08:16.262
and it's going to keep track of every single cell, and the lineage

00:08:16.263 --> 00:08:19.398
of every cell. So at any given time, the computer -- because we

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:22.869
for C. elegans, we know the lineage of every single cell, it knows

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:26.939
what each cell is and what it's going to become. So, you have this

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.243
tool on one hand, and on the other hand, we also collaborated,

00:08:30.244 --> 00:08:34.113
Zhirong and I, with another scientist, he's a microscopist at the

00:08:34.114 --> 00:08:38.184
NIH called Hari Shroff. And Hari developed a microscope that was much more

00:08:38.185 --> 00:08:40.520
powerful than any microscope that existed at the time.

00:08:40.521 --> 00:08:45.558
And allowed us to image the embryo continuously and seamlessly

00:08:45.559 --> 00:08:49.395
for long periods of time without damaging the embryo. That work was

00:08:49.396 --> 00:08:51.898
very important, and in combination with these computer tools,

00:08:51.899 --> 00:08:54.734
allowed us to do things that we couldn't do before. So here's the

00:08:54.735 --> 00:08:57.870
microscope at work, looking also at the development of this

00:08:57.871 --> 00:09:00.039
embryo, but you can see it's happening much faster because

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:03.943
this microscope that Hari developed is 30 times faster than the

00:09:03.944 --> 00:09:07.046
microscopes that we had before. So that all of a sudden, by linking

00:09:07.047 --> 00:09:09.515
our brains, we were able to do more than we could have each

00:09:09.516 --> 00:09:13.553
done individually. And this is actually very important because this

00:09:13.554 --> 00:09:16.489
is how science is done at all levels, even if you were claiming

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.458
to be a scientist and work completely alone, you're actually

00:09:19.459 --> 00:09:24.397
-- you're receiving knowledge from other colleagues that are

00:09:24.398 --> 00:09:26.966
actually colleagues who could be contemporaries, or it could

00:09:26.967 --> 00:09:30.069
be colleagues that passed away, that generated knowledge before

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.139
you came along, and you're building on that knowledge. So

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:36.776
in that sense, science is actually a network of networks, and it's

00:09:36.777 --> 00:09:40.112
a community where we're continuously building on other people's

00:09:40.113 --> 00:09:44.584
knowledge. And in our case here, we were able to then link

00:09:44.585 --> 00:09:47.520
what Zhirong was doing, what I was doing, and what Hari was

00:09:47.521 --> 00:09:50.723
doing, to then generate videos like the one that I'm showing you

00:09:50.724 --> 00:09:55.895
here, of an embryo that is developing in real time, and you can see

00:09:55.896 --> 00:10:00.366
the neurons in green, the nuclei in red. And all of a sudden,

00:10:00.367 --> 00:10:03.236
when this embryo starts moving, you can see that we can actually

00:10:03.237 --> 00:10:06.939
keep track of the individual cells because the microscope that Hari

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:11.811
developed is so efficient and so fast, and we can observe neural

00:10:11.812 --> 00:10:14.780
developmental events that were previously inaccessible.

00:10:14.781 --> 00:10:17.884
And in that way, answer questions that we couldn't answer

00:10:17.885 --> 00:10:22.021
before. So I hope that with these examples, I have illustrated

00:10:22.022 --> 00:10:25.124
to you a little bit how science works, how collaborations work,

00:10:25.125 --> 00:10:28.094
the importance of collaborations in sciences, which is

00:10:28.095 --> 00:10:31.364
fundamental but seldom discussed. And if you're interested

00:10:31.365 --> 00:10:33.699
in these topics and you want to hear more, I invite you to

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:37.136
do a Google search of my last name with "ted blog,"

00:10:37.137 --> 00:10:39.939
I have a blog on this topic, and I also have a Ted-x talk

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:43.743
that discusses in more depth, the importance of basic

00:10:43.744 --> 00:10:47.147
research in biomedicine. Thank you.

